/**

@page swift_particle_lifecycle Particle Lifecycle

Particles are characterized by their attributes (position, velocity, density,
...) as well as by their behaviour in the simulation, i.e. the algorithmic steps.
The latter are taken as input by the Graph Compiler to generate the final C++
application, see @ref swift_graph_compiler "Swift Graph Compiler".
A few examples of particle life cycles can be found under
`/python/swift2/particle`

## SPH leapfrog with fixed interaction radius

A realistic example for the behaviour of an SPH particle is implemented in
```SPHLeapfrogFixedInteractionRadius.py```.


### Declaring particle attributes

This class includes a method to declare the full list of particle attributes
that are needed for the SPH algorithms. The basic structure is the following:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  def declare_sph_particle_attributes(self):
    """
    SPH Hydro particle attributes
    """

    # Baseline particle properties --------------------------------------------------------------
    self.mass         = dastgen2.attributes.Double       ("mass", valid_mantissa_bits=self._mantissa_size)
    self.velocity     = peano4.dastgen2.Peano4DoubleArray("v", "Dimensions", valid_mantissa_bits=self._mantissa_size)
    self.acceleration = peano4.dastgen2.Peano4DoubleArray("a", "Dimensions", valid_mantissa_bits=self._mantissa_size)
    self.data.add_attribute( self.mass )
    self.data.add_attribute( self.velocity )
    self.data.add_attribute( self.acceleration )

    ...
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


This method is then called from the python script where the Swift-Peano project
is implemented for a particular experiment.


### The algorithmic steps for the particle life cycle

The second essential ingredient to build a particle life cycle is to specify the
sequence of algorithmic steps.
As an example within this class, we can find the SPH density loop:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      AlgorithmStep( 
        name                           = "DensityLoopWithConstantSmoothingLength",          
        dependencies                   = AlgorithmStep.Dependencies.NEIGHBOURS, 
        effect                         = AlgorithmStep.Effect.ALTER_LOCAL_STATE, 
        touch_vertex_first_time_kernel = """
                                         ::swift2::kernels::prepareDensityCalculation( localParticles );
                                         """,

        cell_kernel                    = """
                                         ::swift2::kernels::computeDensity( 
                                           localParticles, 
                                           activeParticles,
                                           {}
                                         );
                                         """.format(self._hydroDimensions),

        touch_vertex_last_time_kernel  = """
                                         ::swift2::kernels::endDensityCalculationWithConstantSmoothingLength(
                                             localParticles,
                                             {},
                                             {},
                                             {}
                                             );
                                         """.format(self._hydroDimensions,
                                                    self._eta_factor,
                                                    self._alpha_av),
 
        includes                       = """
                                         #include "Constants.h"
                                         #include "swift2/kernels/ParticleParticleInteraction.h"
                                         #include "swift2/kernels/ParticleSelfInteraction.h"
                                         #include "swift2/kernels/kernel_hydro.h"
                                         #include "swift2/kernels/equation_of_state.h"
                                         """,
      )
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The full life cycle is just a list of such an algorithmic steps.

Some remarks:

  - `dependencies` can distinguish between two options: `NEIGHBOURS` and `SELF`. These
    refer to the two type of operations that are done in the SPH context: either
    the computation depends on the particle itself or it requires contributions
    from neighbours.
  - `effect` has different options depending on the output type of the
    calculation, e.g. `ALTER_LOCAL_STATE` means that only the current particle is
    updated. Other options notably include `CHANGE_POSITION_OR_INTERACTION_RADIUS`,
    which implies that the code needs to resort the particles after this step.
  - `cell_kernel` invokes a compute kernel which depends on the `localParticles`
    and `activeParticles` sets of the PIDT scheme.
  - `touch_vertex_first_time_kernel` invokes a compute kernel which depends
    exclusively on the `localParticles` set of the PIDT scheme when the particle
    is first `touched' during the mesh traversal.
  - `touch_vertex_last_time_kernel` analogous to the previous one.




*/
